"""Editorial question designs for three distinct speculative-fiction niches."""

from review_angle_plans import audit, revise_domain


FANTASY = """history_origins|A Fantasy Tradition in Context|Which earlier narrative practice shaped one named fantasy work?|Trace dated works and documented influence; avoid a single origin story.
famous_examples|A Fantasy World Revisited|What choices make a named work's impossible world legible?|Compare text, maps if authored, and contemporary reception.
hidden_stories|Sources of an Invented World|Whose language, lore, or craft influenced a fantasy setting?|Use attributed creator records and source traditions.
beginner_explainers|How Fantasy Rules Work|What can and cannot happen under one story's stated rules?|Read examples and exceptions before summarizing a system.
advanced_concepts|Power and Consequence in Fantasy|How does an invented power alter social institutions in a novel?|Trace rule, access, cost, and resulting choices.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|An Ambiguous World Rule|Does a seeming contradiction have an explanation in the text?|Compare editions and authorized paratext; leave gaps open.
technology_innovation|Fantasy in an Interactive Medium|Which story possibilities change when a reader can choose actions?|Compare fixed text and an interactive adaptation.
people_personalities|A Fantasy Maker's Revision|Why did a creator change a creature, map, or rule?|Use drafts and attributed accounts rather than inferred intent.
major_events|A Fantasy Work Finds New Readers|What dated publication or adaptation changed its circulation?|Trace editions, access, and situated reception.
how_it_works|A Magical Rule Causes a Plot Turn|Which prior rule makes a later action plausible?|Build a cited chain from setup to consequence.
rise_fall|A Fantasy Trope Reassessed|How did criticism of a particular trope change over time?|Compare dated works and reviews across audiences.
then_vs_now|Two Eras of Fantasy Worldbuilding|What changes in whose viewpoint and whose history a world includes?|Compare bounded texts and publication contexts.
future_developments|A New Fantasy Format|Which documented experiment changes reader agency or world continuity?|Use released work and production constraints, not forecasts.
myths_vs_facts|One Fantasy Formula?|Do counterexamples undermine a claimed universal quest pattern?|Define pattern and test diverse works.
documentary_stories|The Making of a Fantasy World|How did a setting evolve from notes to publication?|Follow dated drafts, illustrations, and editorial decisions.
creative_brief|Inventing a Bounded World|What story question did the creator's brief require the world to support?|Compare stated brief, rules, and finished narrative.
materials_and_tools|Maps, Glossaries, and Drafts|What do worldbuilding artifacts explain and what do they omit?|Check version and authorship against the text.
process_breakdown|Revising a Fantasy Rule|Which draft change solved or created a plot problem?|Compare documented versions and resulting scenes.
composition_choice|Revealing an Impossible Place|When does description withhold or supply a world rule?|Analyze a bounded passage and reader knowledge.
collaborative_authorship|A Shared Fantasy World|Who designed text, art, lore, and adaptation rules?|Use credits, agreements, and version history.
adaptation_across_media|Magic on Page and Screen|What changes when an internal fantasy rule becomes visible action?|Compare passage, script, and finished sequence.
audience_reception|Reading a Fantasy World|Which interpretations are documented across reader communities?|Use dated reviews or studies with sampling limits.
cultural_context|Borrowed Myths in Fantasy|How does a work transform a named source tradition?|Attribute sources and include perspectives from that tradition.
preservation_restoration|A World Across Editions|Which textual or illustrative changes altered a fantasy setting?|Compare identified editions and archival drafts.
rights_and_attribution|Using Folklore in Fantasy|Which story elements are traditional and which have a documented modern author?|Separate public tradition, particular retelling, and rights.
style_comparison|Two Modes of Wonder|How do two works reveal or conceal impossible rules?|Compare matched scenes and narrators.
creative_failure|A Worldbuilding Idea Abandoned|What evidence explains removal of a rule or location?|Use drafts and editorial records without assuming failure.
field_internal_rules|A Rule with Exceptions|Does a stated magical limit hold across key scenes?|Catalog rule, exception, and narrative cost.
field_character_motivation|A Choice under Enchantment|Does magic remove or sharpen a character's agency?|Trace options, knowledge, and consequences.
field_narrative_pacing|When to Reveal a World Rule|How does delayed explanation affect one plot turn?|Map reveal timing against audience knowledge.
field_setting_logic|The Everyday Cost of Magic|How would a stated magical ability affect food, work, or governance in this story?|Test only consequences the work invites.
field_genre_expectations|A Fantasy Promise Redirected|Which familiar expectation does a work establish then revise?|Compare promotional framing, text, and situated reception.
"""

SCIENCE_FICTION = """history_origins|A Speculative Convention Emerges|Which dated scientific or publishing context shaped one science-fiction device?|Compare original work and contemporary sources without claiming one origin.
famous_examples|A Speculative Technology Examined|What premise does a named work actually require?|Read its stated mechanism and narrative consequences.
hidden_stories|Science Fiction Beyond the Canon|Whose speculative work was missed by a narrow genre history?|Use dated publications, translations, and reception.
beginner_explainers|The Counterfactual in Science Fiction|Which one change organizes a story's imagined world?|Identify premise, baseline, and consequences.
advanced_concepts|A Model of Society in Fiction|What institutional effects follow from a speculative technology?|Trace access, power, constraints, and dissent in the text.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|An Unexplained Future Device|Which mechanism does the story explain and which does it leave open?|Separate textual fact from fan speculation.
technology_innovation|A Real Tool and Its Fictional Double|How does a fictional device depart from contemporary science?|Compare dated scientific sources with the work, not later inventions.
people_personalities|A Writer's Speculative Revision|How did a draft change the scientific premise?|Use drafts, letters, and attributed interviews.
major_events|A Science-Fiction Debate in Print|Which editorial or publishing decision shifted a theme's circulation?|Trace dated magazines, books, and responses.
how_it_works|A Premise Generates Plot|What consequence follows from one speculative assumption?|Map causal steps and note where the work skips them.
rise_fall|An Old Future Revisited|What does an obsolete forecast reveal about its original context?|Compare period expectations with later evidence without grading prophecy.
then_vs_now|A Device Across Two Eras|How did two works imagine the same technological problem differently?|Match premise and compare social assumptions.
future_developments|A Future Story as Scenario|Which present concern does a near-future narrative explore?|Treat story as thought experiment, not prediction.
myths_vs_facts|Science Fiction Predicted This?|What did a specific work describe before a real technology existed?|Date publication, define similarity, and avoid causal claims.
documentary_stories|Tracing a Speculative Idea|How did one premise move through drafts, publication, and adaptation?|Follow attributed, dated artifacts.
creative_brief|The Thought Experiment's Boundary|What rule makes this imagined future distinct?|Compare author brief and the finished work's exceptions.
materials_and_tools|Research Behind a Future World|Which sources informed a fictional device or society?|Verify cited research and date it to composition.
process_breakdown|Revising a Speculative Premise|What changed between an outline and published version?|Compare versioned text and editorial notes.
composition_choice|Explaining Without Lecturing|How does a scene disclose an unfamiliar technology?|Analyze action, dialogue, and reader knowledge.
collaborative_authorship|A Shared Future|Who developed scientific advice, writing, visual design, and adaptation?|Use credits and documented contributions.
adaptation_across_media|A Future on Page and Screen|Which speculative rules survive an adaptation?|Compare source, script, design, and final sequence.
audience_reception|Reading the Future Differently|How did audiences in different periods understand a work's premise?|Use situated reviews and accessible editions.
cultural_context|Whose Future Is Imagined?|Whose labor, location, and power relations does a story foreground or omit?|Analyze the work and attributed criticism without universalizing.
preservation_restoration|A Speculative Work Across Editions|Which revisions changed the imagined technology or society?|Compare dated versions and publisher records.
rights_and_attribution|An Idea and Its Expression|Who is credited for a shared speculative device or universe?|Separate general premise, particular expression, and contractual rights.
style_comparison|Two Versions of the Future|How do two works turn the same scientific uncertainty into different stories?|Compare explicit assumptions and narrative scale.
creative_failure|A Discarded Future|Why was an earlier speculative mechanism changed?|Use draft or production record rather than perceived plausibility.
field_internal_rules|Consistency of a Future Device|Which capabilities and costs remain stable across scenes?|Check setup, exceptions, and later payoffs.
field_character_motivation|A Human Choice in a New System|What does technology make possible or compulsory for a character?|Trace incentives, access, and alternatives.
field_narrative_pacing|When the Premise Becomes Clear|How does the work time explanation of its new world?|Map reveal, confusion, and subsequent payoff.
field_setting_logic|A Speculative System's Infrastructure|What energy, labor, or institutions would its stated technology require?|Test internally supported consequences and mark unknowns.
field_genre_expectations|Hard Science or Social Speculation|Which expectations does a work invite and then challenge?|Use its text, publication framing, and dated reception.
"""

HORROR = """history_origins|A Horror Motif in Context|Which dated cultural or literary source shaped one frightening motif?|Trace particular works and avoid a universal fear story.
famous_examples|A Famous Fright Reexamined|How does a named scene create threat before showing it?|Analyze image or prose, sound, timing, and audience information.
hidden_stories|Makers of a Frightening Scene|Whose design, sound, performance, or writing contributed to it?|Check credits and production records.
beginner_explainers|Dread, Shock, and Aftermath|How do three distinct effects operate in one horror work?|Identify setup, event, and consequence in bounded passages.
advanced_concepts|Horror and Social Anxiety|Which contextual reading is supported by a specific work and period?|Compare text, contemporary debate, and alternative interpretations.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|An Ambiguous Threat|What does the work deliberately leave unexplained?|Separate narrative ambiguity from missing archival evidence.
technology_innovation|A New Horror Technique|How did one sound, makeup, camera, or game tool shape a scene?|Document actual production method and audience effect cautiously.
people_personalities|A Creator's Fear Design|Which documented choice changed how a threat appears?|Compare drafts, storyboards, and credited testimony.
major_events|A Horror Release and Reaction|How did a specific release meet censorship or public response?|Use dated rulings, reviews, and exhibition records.
how_it_works|Suspense from Limited Knowledge|Who knows what before a frightening reveal?|Map character and audience information scene by scene.
rise_fall|A Monster's Changing Meaning|How did a particular figure's interpretation change across adaptations?|Compare versions and situated criticism.
then_vs_now|The Same Fear in Two Periods|How do two works stage a related threat under different norms?|Compare form, context, and documented reception.
future_developments|An Emerging Horror Format|Which released experiment changes viewer control or proximity?|Analyze specific work and avoid forecasting fear universally.
myths_vs_facts|The Scariest Scene for Everyone?|What can reviews or audience studies actually show about reaction?|Specify sample, place, and selection limits.
documentary_stories|Making a Horror Sequence|How did concept, practical effects, and edit create a finished scare?|Follow dated designs, tests, and cuts.
creative_brief|Defining the Threat|What was a production asked to make visible or uncertain?|Compare brief, draft, and released work.
materials_and_tools|Building a Creature or Setting|How do material effects and sound sources shape a threat?|Identify tools and distinguish production fact from screen illusion.
process_breakdown|A Scare Across Drafts|Which change altered timing or stakes in a scene?|Compare drafts, rehearsals, and final cut.
composition_choice|The Frame Outside the Threat|How does offscreen space invite a viewer to anticipate danger?|Analyze a bounded sequence and alternatives.
collaborative_authorship|Who Designed the Fear?|How did writer, performer, designer, editor, and composer contribute?|Use credits and production records, not single-author myth.
adaptation_across_media|Fear from Page to Screen|What changes when a described threat becomes visible and audible?|Compare source passage, script, and finished scene.
audience_reception|Different Viewers, Different Fears|Which reactions to a work are documented across audiences?|Avoid universal psychological claims from selected reviews.
cultural_context|A Threat with Local Meaning|Which social context changes how a horror motif is read?|Use local criticism and distinguish work from general culture.
preservation_restoration|A Horror Film's Missing Version|What footage or sound was cut, restored, or lost?|Compare archival elements, censorship records, and releases.
rights_and_attribution|A Monster's Many Makers|Which elements derive from a source and which from adaptation?|Trace credits, licenses, and specific designs.
style_comparison|Two Routes to Unease|How do restraint and explicit depiction work in matched scenes?|Compare timing, framing, sound, and context.
creative_failure|A Scare Removed|What evidence explains an abandoned effect or scene?|Use tests and production accounts rather than assume viewers disliked it.
field_internal_rules|A Threat's Limits|What can the monster or force do, and where does the work break its rule?|Catalog stated constraints and exceptions.
field_character_motivation|A Choice under Threat|What information and options did a character actually have?|Trace decision point instead of calling behavior irrational.
field_narrative_pacing|Waiting for the Threat|How long does a work sustain uncertainty before a reveal?|Map duration, false alarms, and aftermath.
field_setting_logic|A Dangerous Place's Rules|How does geography or access make the threat plausible?|Track entrances, sight lines, escape routes, and continuity.
field_genre_expectations|A Horror Promise Subverted|Which familiar cue is set up and then denied?|Compare work, marketing, and documented reception.
"""


if __name__ == "__main__":
    revise_domain("373", "373_fantasy", FANTASY,
                  "Fantasy fiction as invented worlds and impossible premises whose rules, choices, sources, and reception can be examined in particular works.",
                  "identified editions, author drafts, source traditions, production notes, archive records, attributed criticism, and situated reader accounts",
                  "Keep invented-world facts inside the specified work and edition. Attribute borrowed traditions and distinguish interpretation from author intent.")
    revise_domain("374", "374_science_fiction", SCIENCE_FICTION,
                  "Science fiction as narrative thought experiments that connect a speculative change to social, technical, and personal consequences.",
                  "identified texts and editions, dated scientific context, author drafts, publication and adaptation records, and situated criticism",
                  "Separate fictional premise from current scientific fact and prediction. Define the work, edition, and publication context before comparing real technologies.")
    revise_domain("375", "375_horror_fiction", HORROR,
                  "Horror fiction and film as designed experiences of dread, threat, shock, and aftermath, read through form, production, context, and varied audience responses.",
                  "identified works and versions, scripts, production and effects records, censorship documents, dated reviews, and bounded audience research",
                  "Do not claim a work frightens everyone or diagnose a viewer. Separate observable craft, documented response, and interpretation of social meaning.")
    rows = audit()
    print(f"Plans: {len(rows)}; reviewed question designs: {sum(r['status'] == 'editorial_question_reviewed' for r in rows)}")
